  <!-- 4. Introduction -->
  <h2>4. Introduction</h2>
  <h3>4.1 Background and Context</h3>
  <p>The modern global financial ecosystem is fundamentally reliant on high-reliability, low-latency, and secure digital banking platforms. Financial institutions process millions of critical consumer and commercial transactions every second. A single software defect, unhandled boundary exception, or security loophole can cause devastating monetary losses, regulatory fines, and permanent reputational damage. In this high-stakes environment, quality assurance cannot remain an afterthought relegated to manual user acceptance testing at the end of development cycles. Instead, financial engineering organizations must adopt a <b>Comprehensive Test Strategy</b> seamlessly embedded within a <b>DevSecOps</b> continuous delivery pipeline.</p>

  <h3>4.2 Alignment with UN Sustainable Development Goal 9 (SDG 9)</h3>
  <p>This project is designed in direct alignment with <b>UN SDG 9: Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure</b>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Target 9.1 (Resilient Infrastructure):</b> Engineering a banking platform backed by deterministic automated test gates that guarantee system uptime, transactional consistency, and data resilience against sudden operational spikes or cyber intrusions.</li>
    <li><b>Target 9.4 (Sustainable & Resource-Efficient Technology):</b> Implementing a zero-external-dependency Node.js architecture packaged within an ultra-compact Alpine Linux container (136 MB). This minimizes memory overhead, reduces cloud compute requirements, and curbs digital infrastructure energy consumption.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- 5. Problem Definition and Scope -->
  <h2>5. Problem Definition and Scope</h2>
  <h3>5.1 Problem Statement</h3>
  <p>Digital banking applications face critical challenges that conventional development methodologies fail to resolve: late-stage defect discovery, inadequate boundary testing, unmitigated supply-chain risks, and security configuration drift. Traditional manual testing leaves critical attack vectors unverified—such as SQL injection parameter tampering, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), negative value transfer manipulation, and unauthorized overdrafts.</p>

  <h3>5.2 Project Scope & Objectives</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Functional Banking Portal (SecureBank):</b> Multi-account balance tracking, authenticated funds transfer with PIN verification, and an immutable transaction history ledger.</li>
    <li><b>Comprehensive Automated Test Strategy:</b> 10 automated test suites verifying mathematical balance integrity, currency formatting, SQL injection filtering, XSS neutralization, overdraft enforcement, and DoS buffer protection.</li>
    <li><b>Containerization & Pipeline Automation:</b> Multi-stage non-root Docker build orchestrated via an 8-stage Jenkins pipeline with SonarQube SAST and Trivy vulnerability quality gates.</li>
    <li><b>Infrastructure as Code:</b> Codified AWS VPC, EC2 instance with IMDSv2, and encrypted S3 audit storage using HashiCorp Terraform.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <!-- 6. Design and Methodology -->
  <h2>6. Design and Methodology</h2>
  <h3>6.1 System Architecture</h3>
  <p>SecureBank follows a tiered, modular architectural pattern enforcing defense-in-depth:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Presentation Layer:</b> High-performance semantic dashboard utilizing HTML5, CSS3, and DOM-safe vanilla JavaScript. All dynamic rendering is performed using text nodes to prevent DOM-based XSS attacks.</li>
    <li><b>Application / API Layer:</b> Built on native Node.js HTTP primitives. All endpoints enforce strict regular expression allow-lists, payload size capping (4 KB), and atomic transactional state management.</li>
    <li><b>Data / Audit Layer:</b> In-memory transaction ledger maintaining chronological consistency, backed by versioned, AES-256 encrypted cloud storage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6.2 DevSecOps Automated Delivery Workflow</h3>
  <pre>
Developer Commit & Push (main / dev branches)
         │
         ▼
GitHub Webhook Trigger
         │
         ▼
Jenkins Declarative Pipeline:
  Stage 1: Checkout SCM
  Stage 2: Comprehensive Automated Test Suite (Functional, Security & Boundary)
  Stage 3: SonarQube SAST Code Analysis (Zero Security Hotspots Quality Gate)
  Stage 4: Trivy Filesystem & Secret Audit (Zero Leaked Credentials)
  Stage 5: Multi-Stage Docker Container Build (node:20-alpine, USER node)
  Stage 6: Trivy Container Image Scan (Quality Gate: Fail on CRITICAL CVEs)
  Stage 7: Push Image to Docker Hub Registry
  Stage 8: Automated SSH Deployment to AWS EC2 Instance
  Stage 9: Automated Post-Deployment Smoke Healthcheck (/health)
  </pre>

  <!-- 7. Implementation and Results -->
  <h2>7. Implementation and Results</h2>
  <h3>7.1 Website & API Implementation</h3>
  <p>The core application is implemented in <code>src/app.js</code> and <code>src/server.js</code> with endpoints for account status, transfers, and live test strategy execution.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Method</th><th>Description</th><th>Security Gate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>/health</code></td><td>GET</td><td>System health and SDG 9 indicator</td><td>Public probe returning status 200</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/api/accounts</code></td><td>GET</td><td>Returns active accounts & balances</td><td>Sanitized JSON, no credentials exposed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/api/transactions</code></td><td>GET</td><td>Immutable chronological ledger</td><td>Read-only, timestamped records</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/api/transfer</code></td><td>POST</td><td>Executes funds transfer between accounts</td><td>PIN required, balance checked, regex validated</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>/api/tests/run</code></td><td>POST</td><td>Executes live Comprehensive Test Suite</td><td>In-app test runner executing 9 verification gates</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>7.2 Containerization & Docker</h3>
  <p>The application is packaged in a hardened multi-stage Dockerfile based on <code>node:20-alpine</code>. The container runs under non-privileged system user <code>USER node</code>, includes an internal health check (<code>wget /health</code>), and operates with a total image size of only 136 MB.</p>

  <h3>7.3 GitHub Repository Verification</h3>
  <p>Source code is maintained on GitHub under repository: <a href="https://github.com/parvs2006-hash/vaultline">https://github.com/parvs2006-hash/vaultline</a>, with both <code>main</code> and <code>dev</code> branches synchronized and active.</p>

  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <!-- 8. Security Assessment and Basic Vulnerability Report -->
  <h2>8. Security Assessment and Basic Vulnerability Report</h2>
  <h3>8.1 Security Scanning Summary</h3>
  <p>Security analysis was executed using SonarQube (Static Application Security Testing) and Aqua Security Trivy (Dependency, secret, and container vulnerability scanning).</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Tool</th><th>Scan Target</th><th>Identified Issue / Threat</th><th>Severity</th><th>Applied Mitigation</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Trivy fs</td><td><code>terraform/main.tf</code></td><td>Potential administrative SSH port 22 open globally</td><td>High</td><td>Parameterized <code>admin_cidr</code> variable restricting access to admin IP only.</td><td><span class="badge badge-pass">Mitigated</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Trivy fs</td><td><code>terraform/main.tf</code></td><td>AWS EC2 metadata service SSRF credential theft (IMDSv1)</td><td>High</td><td>Enforced IMDSv2 via <code>http_tokens = "required"</code> in Terraform.</td><td><span class="badge badge-pass">Mitigated</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>SonarQube</td><td><code>src/app.js</code></td><td>Buffer exhaustion Denial of Service via large HTTP bodies</td><td>Medium</td><td>Implemented 4 KB request stream size limiter with HTTP 413 rejection.</td><td><span class="badge badge-pass">Mitigated</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>SonarQube</td><td><code>public/app.js</code></td><td>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via unescaped string injection</td><td>High</td><td>Avoided <code>innerHTML</code>; dynamic content rendered strictly via text nodes.</td><td><span class="badge badge-pass">Mitigated</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Trivy image</td><td><code>Dockerfile</code></td><td>Container breakout vulnerability under root user</td><td>High</td><td>Switched execution to non-root <code>USER node</code> on Alpine base.</td><td><span class="badge badge-pass">Mitigated</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <!-- 9. Analysis and Interpretation -->
  <h2>9. Analysis and Interpretation</h2>
  <h3>9.1 Automated Test Execution Results</h3>
  <p>Execution of the automated test runner via <code>node --test test/bank.test.js</code> yielded a <b>100% pass rate</b> across all 10 test suites in under 175 ms:</p>
  <pre>
TAP version 13
# Subtest: health check returns ok with SDG 9 metadata ............. PASS (37.5 ms)
# Subtest: security headers are strictly enforced ................... PASS (3.4 ms)
# Subtest: retrieve all active accounts with initial balances ....... PASS (1.9 ms)
# Subtest: execute valid funds transfer with balance deduction ..... PASS (7.6 ms)
# Subtest: reject negative or zero transfer amounts ................ PASS (6.0 ms)
# Subtest: reject transfer exceeding available balance (overdraft) . PASS (1.7 ms)
# Subtest: reject transfer when origin and target are identical .... PASS (3.4 ms)
# Subtest: reject SQL injection payload in account number .......... PASS (2.8 ms)
# Subtest: reject transfer with invalid security PIN ............... PASS (1.7 ms)
# Subtest: run in-app test strategy engine and verify 100% pass ..... PASS (2.4 ms)
1..10
# tests 10 | pass 10 | fail 0 | cancelled 0 | duration_ms 170.5 ms
  </pre>

  <div class="page-break"></div>

  <!-- 10. Conclusion and Future Scope -->
  <h2>10. Conclusion and Future Scope</h2>
  <p>The SecureBank CIAP microproject successfully demonstrates how a Comprehensive Test Strategy integrated within an automated DevSecOps pipeline delivers resilient, high-assurance digital financial systems in alignment with UN SDG 9. Future enhancements include implementing Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), transitioning from in-memory state to an encrypted Amazon RDS PostgreSQL cluster, and integrating Chaos Engineering into the pipeline.</p>

